  "notes": "# Divergent Association Task\n\n## What it is\nA brief creativity task where participants generate words that should be as unrelated as possible, then receive a score based on the average semantic distance among those words.\n\n## Why it matters\nDAT adds a modern, automation-friendly divergent-thinking measure to the canonical registry. It contributes a narrow but useful creativity-performance seam that complements broader trait frameworks and symbolic systems.\n\n## How it is administered and scored\n- participants provide 10 single nouns intended to be maximally different\n- public scoring retains the first seven valid responses for the core semantic-distance calculation\n- semantic distance is computed pairwise across the retained words\n- the DAT score is a transformed average distance score, where higher values indicate more remote association structure in responses\n\n## Validity and limits\nInitial studies report convergent validity with established creativity tasks and meaningful short-term test-retest reliability. At the same time, DAT remains a constrained task measure: strategy choice, language factors, verbal fluency, and instruction adherence can shift scores.\n\n## What it is good for\n- scalable divergent-thinking measurement in research contexts\n- low-overhead creativity-task baselining\n- complementing broader multi-framework synthesis when used conservatively\n\n## What it is weaker at\n- stable personality interpretation\n- whole-person creativity diagnosis\n- high-stakes standalone screening decisions\n\n## Common misuse\nTreating DAT as equivalent to a broad personality trait or as a complete measure of creativity rather than a narrow divergent-association task signal.\n\nThe DAT FAQ is explicit that the task captures only one sliver of creativity. That boundary should remain visible anywhere the score is brought into broader person-model synthesis.\n"
